    "body": "**TLDR:** `Rubric.Config.truncation_penalty` adds a fixed penalty to a truncated rollout's reward-fn score, so truncation ranks below finishing without losing partial credit.\n\n**Problem**\n1. Rewards can give partial credit, e.g. the fraction of tests that pass.\n2. `truncation_reward` replaces that score with a constant: a truncated rollout that passed 9 of 10 tests scores like one that passed 0.\n3. Leaving it None scores a truncated rollout exactly like the same rollout finished.\n\n**Solution**\n1. `truncation_penalty` (e.g. -0.2) is added once to the weighted score of a truncated rollout.\n2. It shows in `reward_breakdown` as `truncation_penalty`; setting it together with `truncation_reward` raises.\n3. Unchanged: finished and errored rollouts, and `truncation_reward`.\n\n**Example**\n```text\nOne truncated rollout that passed 9 of 10 tests (reward fn score 0.9):\ntruncation_reward=0.0      reward 0.0   same as passing 0 of 10\nboth None (default)        reward 0.9   same as finishing\ntruncation_penalty=-0.2    reward 0.7   breakdown {\"tests\": 0.9, \"truncation_penalty\": -0.2}\n```\n\nNOTE: no in-tree recipe sets it yet; it is for rewards with partial credit. Validation shares the rubric, so it sees the penalty too.\nNOTE: `rollout_reward/component/truncation_penalty` logs the constant penalty, like `errored` does today; the rate is `rollout/truncation_rate`.\n\n**Test plan**\n```bash\npytest tests/rl/unit_tests/cpu/test_rubric.py   # 6 passed\npytest tests/rl/unit_tests/cpu                  # 485 passed, 9 skipped\n```\n- On main, 5 of the 6 fail. `test_truncation_reward_still_short_circuits_the_reward_fns` pins unchanged behavior and passes on main by design.\n- The reward fn has weight 2.0, so a penalty wrongly scaled by the weight sum fails; the errored rollout runs with `error_reward=None`, so a penalty applied to any non-completed status fails.\n",
